    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#imad-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Imad al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imad_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyids came from Daylam, the mountainous region south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> whose hardy infantry had long resisted outside rule and whose men served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercenary">mercenaries</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> in the tenth century. Their father, Buya, was remembered as a man of modest origin, by tradition a fisherman, and his three sons, Ali, Hasan and Ahmad, rose as officers in the service of Mardavij, founder of the Ziyarid dynasty. In 934 Ali defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>'s governor Yaqut and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fars">Fars</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> his base. After Mardavij was murdered in 935, Hasan fought for years to secure Ray, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#isfahan">Isfahan</a> and the Jibal, while Ahmad advanced into Khuzistan and, in December 945, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The caliph al-Mustakfi gave the brothers the honorific titles by which they are known, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a>, Rukn al-Dawla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>, 'support', 'pillar' and 'strengthener' of the state; a few weeks later Mu'izz al-Dawla had him blinded and deposed and installed al-Muti in his place. The Buyids thus became masters of the Abbasid capital and of most of western Iran within a dozen years. Their rise belongs to what Vladimir Minorsky called the 'Iranian intermezzo', the period between Arab and Turkish domination when Iranian dynasties ruled much of the eastern caliphate.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#baghdad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baghdad.jpg" alt="Baghdad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5628442718_b10fc2c47f_o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USACE HQ , JIM GORDAN, CIV, USACE, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyid lands, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> through Khuzistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fars">Fars</a> to the Jibal, included some of the richest agricultural and urban regions of the Islamic world, but they had suffered from decades of disorder before the brothers arrived. The Iraqi countryside in particular had declined, and the Buyids' reliance on <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a></em> grants, which assigned the tax revenue of land to soldiers in place of salaries, was blamed by contemporaries such as the historian Miskawayh for further damaging cultivation, since the holders had little interest in maintaining irrigation. Fars, the family's heartland, fared better, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> invested in it, building the Band-e <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amir">Amir</a> dam on the Kur river, which irrigated the plain of Marvdasht. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> remained a great city and a centre of trade and scholarship, though torn by riots between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam">Shia</a> quarters and between soldiers and townspeople. Trade through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, with Siraf as its leading port, linked the Buyid lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-africa">East Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>. The Buyids were Shia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> introduced public mourning for Husayn on Ashura and celebration of the feast of Ghadir in Baghdad in 963. Under their rule Twelver Shia scholarship flourished in Baghdad, with al-Shaykh al-Mufid, al-Sharif al-Murtada and al-Sharif al-Radi, compiler of the <em>Nahj al-Balagha</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#muizz-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Mu'izz al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mu%27izz_al-Dawla_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyid armies combined two very different forces: Daylamite infantry, the dynasty's own countrymen, fighting with spears, javelins and large shields, and Turkish cavalry, mostly slave soldiers or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#freedmen">freedmen</a>, who provided the mobility the Daylamites lacked. The rivalry between these groups, over pay, status and religion, was a constant source of instability; in 974 the Turkish commander Sabuktakin rebelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>'s son Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar, and the mutiny helped draw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a>. The brothers and their heirs fought on many fronts: against the Hamdanids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mosul">Mosul</a> in the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#samanid-empire">Samanids</a> of Khurasan and the Ziyarids of Gurgan in the east, and rival dynasties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kerman">Kerman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oman">Oman</a>, which Adud al-Dawla subdued in the late 960s. Adud al-Dawla's campaigns were the most successful: in 977–978 he defeated his cousin Bakhtiyar, who was killed at the battle of Qasr al-Jiss in 978, and in 979 he took Mosul from the Hamdanids, while his brother Mu'ayyad al-Dawla drove the Ziyarid ruler Qabus out of Gurgan. Adud also received the rebel Byzantine general Bardas Skleros, who fled to Baghdad, and held him as a bargaining counter with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>. After 983, however, Buyid armies were used mainly against other Buyids.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Adud al-Dawla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adud_al-Dawla_medallion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Smithsonian, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyids ruled as a family confederation rather than a single state. Each brother, and later each son, held his own territory, and the most senior was recognised as head of the family: first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a> until his death in 949, then Rukn al-Dawla at Ray. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> remained the formal source of legitimate authority for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims, invested the Buyid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir">emirs</a>, and was named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khutba">Friday prayer</a>, but he lost all real power: the emir controlled the army, the treasury and appointments, and the caliph lived on a pension. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, who united the Buyid lands from 977 until his death in 983, styled himself <em>shahanshah</em>, '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#negus-nagast">king of kings</a>', reviving the old Persian royal title, and he became the first ruler to have his own name mentioned after the caliph's in the Friday prayer in Baghdad, a break with tradition. Administration followed Abbasid models, run by capable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">viziers</a> drawn from the secretarial class, such as Ibn al-Amid, vizier of Rukn al-Dawla, and al-Sahib ibn Abbad, vizier of Mu'ayyad al-Dawla and Fakhr al-Dawla at Ray. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a></em> system spread under the Buyids and later became the basis of military administration under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. Adud al-Dawla also founded the Adudi hospital in Baghdad, opened around 982.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shiraz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shiraz.jpg" alt="Shiraz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darvaze_Ghoran11.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">آرش (Arche), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Buyid century was one of the most brilliant periods of Islamic intellectual life, sometimes called a renaissance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>. Buyid princes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">viziers</a> were generous patrons: the astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sufism">Sufi</a> dedicated his <em>Book of Fixed Stars</em> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, the poet al-Mutanabbi visited his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiraz">Shiraz</a>, the grammarian Abu Ali al-Farisi wrote for him, and Miskawayh, librarian and historian, wrote his universal history <em>The Experiences of the Nations</em> and works of ethics under Buyid patronage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> served the Buyid emir Shams al-Dawla of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hamadan">Hamadan</a> as vizier, and al-Sahib ibn Abbad gathered scholars, including the Mu'tazili theologian Abd al-Jabbar, at Ray. The Buyids also shaped the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam">Shia Islam</a>: under their protection Twelver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shiism">Shiism</a> developed its theology and law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the public rituals of Ashura and Ghadir took root. Politically, they created the pattern of a secular sultan ruling in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>'s name, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> inherited, and they spread the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iqta">iqta</a></em> as the basis of military finance across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a>. Their revival of the title <em>shahanshah</em> and of Persian royal imagery reflected a growing Iranian identity within Islamic civilisation that would later flower in Persian literature.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tughril"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughril.jpg" alt="Tughril" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tughril">Tughril</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seljuk_Sultan_Tugrul_Bey_sitting_on_his_throne._Topkap%C4%B1_Saray%C4%B1_M%C3%BCzesi_1653-3_(304_B).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The family structure that had allowed the Buyids to conquer so quickly became the main cause of their decline. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>'s death in 983 his sons Samsam al-Dawla, Sharaf al-Dawla and Baha al-Dawla fought for the inheritance; Sharaf al-Dawla took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 987, and Baha al-Dawla, ruling from 989 to 1012, reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fars">Fars</a> but could not restore Adud's authority. Rival branches held Ray, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hamadan">Hamadan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#isfahan">Isfahan</a>, and local dynasties such as the Kakuyids and the Kurdish Hasanwayhids gained ground. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphs</a>, above all al-Qadir, reasserted their own religious authority and championed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy. In 1029 Mahmud of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> took Ray and deposed Majd al-Dawla. Abu Kalijar held Fars and Iraq until 1048, but his successors were too weak to resist the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. In December 1055 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tughril">Tughril Beg</a> of the Seljuk Empire entered Baghdad, was recognised as sultan by the caliph al-Qa'im, and imprisoned the last Buyid emir of Iraq, al-Malik al-Rahim. The last Buyid prince in Fars, Abu Mansur Fulad Sutun, was overthrown and killed by Fadluya, chief of the Shabankara <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kurds">Kurds</a>, in 1062, ending the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Buyid dynasty, Daylamite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam">Shia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#emir">emirs</a> who ruled western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iraq">Iraq</a> and controlled the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphs</a> from 945 to 1055, created the model of a secular ruler beside a powerless caliph and presided over a remarkable flowering of Islamic science, philosophy and Shia scholarship.</em></p>

